<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n32920" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">orchis</span></b> , is, f., =
			<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὄρχις</span>
		<div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>A plant</i>, <i>a kind of olive</i>,
				<i><span class="en">Col.
				5, 8, 4</span></i>; <i><span class="en">id. Arb. 17, 3</span></i>;
				<i><span class="en">Pall. Febr. 18,
			4</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>Another plant</i>, so called from the shape of its
			roots, <i>the orchis</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">mirabilis est orchis herba, sive serapias,
					gemina radice testiculis simili,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Plin.
					26, 10, 62, § 95</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
</div>